    @section append-include-path %append-include-path

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%append-include-path</tt> <em>dir1[:dir2...]</em>

    @par Command Line:
    n/a

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    n/a

    @par Description:
    Appends the given directories to qore's include path; also performs environment variable substitution.  Only directories that exist and
    are readable are added to the include path.

    @see @ref append-module-path "%append-module-path"

    <hr>
    @section append-module-path %append-module-path

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%append-module-path</tt> <em>dir1[:dir2...]</em>  (legacy form)<br>
    <tt>%%append-module-path "</tt><em>path</em><tt>"</tt>  (new-style form)

    @par Command Line:
    n/a

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    n/a

    @par Description:
    Appends a directory to the module search path.  Two forms are supported; the scanner
    dispatches on the first non-whitespace character of the argument:

    - **Legacy form** (unquoted, colon-separated): appends the given directories to
      qore's <em>process-global</em> module path; performs \c $VAR-style environment
      variable substitution. Only directories that exist and are readable are added
      to the path. Shared across every @ref Qore::Program "Program" in the process.
      \n\n
    - **New-style form** (quoted, single path): appends the given path to the
      <em>current Program's</em>
      @ref prepend-module-path "per-Program appended module path list". Supports \c ${NAME} expansion against a table of predefined macros
      (\c SCRIPT_DIR, \c SCRIPT_NAME, \c SCRIPT_PATH, \c PROGRAM_DIR, \c QORE_VERSION,
      \c QORE_PREFIX, \c QORE_MODULE_VERSION) and, as a fallback, the process
      environment. Unknown macros / unset env vars raise a parse error — there is no
      silent empty-string substitution. \c \\$ escapes a literal \c $. Macros win over
      env vars on conflict.

    In the effective search-path precedence (highest → lowest):
    -# Per-Program @ref prepend-module-path "%prepend-module-path" paths
    -# Process \c QORE_MODULE_DIR entries and legacy <tt>%%append-module-path</tt> additions
    -# Compiled-in default module paths
    -# Per-Program new-style <tt>%%append-module-path</tt> paths

    @par Examples:
    @code{.py}
# legacy form — process-global, colon-separated
%append-module-path /opt/qore/qlib:/var/opt/qore/qlib

# new form — per-Program, quoted, single path, macro expansion
%append-module-path "${HOME}/.qore-modules-fallback"
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 0.8.6 (legacy form); new quoted form since %Qore 3.0

    @see @ref prepend-module-path "%prepend-module-path", @ref no-module-path-directives "%no-module-path-directives", @ref append-include-path "%append-include-path"

    <hr>
    @section prepend-module-path %prepend-module-path

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%prepend-module-path "</tt><em>path</em><tt>"</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    n/a

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    n/a

    @par Description:
    Prepends a directory to the current @ref Qore::Program "Program" object's module
    search path. Takes a single <em>quoted</em> path with \c ${NAME} macro + env-var
    expansion. Multiple prepends stack in most-recently-added order (the last
    <tt>%%prepend-module-path</tt> parsed sits at the head of the search list).

    Prepended paths are consulted <em>before</em> \c QORE_MODULE_DIR and before
    the built-in default module directories — use this when a program must find a
    specific vendored / side-deployed copy of a module regardless of the process
    environment.

    Expansion happens at <em>parse time</em> on the parsing host; the expanded path
    is persisted into @ref aot_builds "AOT-compiled artifacts" and re-applied by the
    AOT runtime loader before any module-dependency loads fire. This makes the
    directive the recommended way to declare module search paths in AOT-compiled
    binaries — no host-side \c MM.addModuleDir() call or \c QORE_MODULE_DIR launcher
    hack required.

    @par Predefined macros:
    | Macro | Value at parse time |
    |-------|---------------------|
    | <tt>${SCRIPT_DIR}</tt> | absolute directory of the file containing the directive |
    | <tt>${SCRIPT_NAME}</tt> | basename of the file containing the directive |
    | <tt>${SCRIPT_PATH}</tt> | absolute path of the file containing the directive |
    | <tt>${PROGRAM_DIR}</tt> | absolute directory of the top-level program file |
    | <tt>${QORE_VERSION}</tt> | \%Qore library version string (e.g. \c "3.0.0") |
    | <tt>${QORE_PREFIX}</tt> | \%Qore install prefix (e.g. \c "/usr") |
    | <tt>${QORE_MODULE_VERSION}</tt> | module API version (e.g. \c "2.0") |

    Anything not in this table falls through to the process environment. \c \\$ escapes
    a literal \c $. Unknown macros / unset env vars raise a parse error.

    \c ${SCRIPT_*} macros require the directive to appear in an on-disk file; code parsed
    from an in-memory buffer (e.g. <tt>Program::parse(string)</tt>) raises a parse error
    unless @ref Qore::Program::setScriptPath() "setScriptPath()" was called first.

    Subprograms inherit their parent's prepend/append lists (see
    @ref Qore::Program "Program" inheritance semantics).

    @par Examples:
    @code{.py}
# deployment-style: locate vendored modules relative to an APP_DIR env var
%prepend-module-path "${APP_DIR}/lib/modules"
%prepend-module-path "${APP_DIR}/qlib"
%prepend-module-path "${APP_DIR}/user/modules"
%requires AppInterfaceCore

# script-local vendored modules
%prepend-module-path "${SCRIPT_DIR}/qlib"
%requires MyVendoredHelper
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @see @ref append-module-path "%append-module-path", @ref no-module-path-directives "%no-module-path-directives", @ref requires "%requires"

    <hr>
    @section assume-global %assume-global

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%assume-global</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    n/a

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    n/a

    @par Description:
    Resets the default %Qore behavior of assuming global variable scope when variables are first referenced if no @ref my "my" or @ref our "our" is present; use after @ref assume-local "%assume-local" to reset the default parsing behavior. This parse option is also set with @ref old-style "%old-style"

    @since %Qore 0.8.4

    @see @ref assume-local "%assume-local"

    <hr>
    @section assume-local %assume-local

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%assume-local</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-assume-local</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_ASSUME_LOCAL

    @par Description:
    Assume local variable scope when variables are first referenced if no @ref my "my" or @ref our "our" is present. When used with @ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs", local variables without @ref my "my" must be declared with a data type restriction (can be any). This parse option is set by default with @ref new-style "%new-style" and is the opposite of @ref assume-global "%assume-global"

    @since %Qore 0.8.1

    @see @ref assume-global "%assume-global"

    <hr>
    @section broken-auto-cast %broken-auto-cast

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-auto-cast</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-auto-cast</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::QoreParseOptions::BROKEN_AUTO_CAST (string name: \c "broken-auto-cast")

    @par Description:
    Accept the misleading no-op casts \c cast<hash<auto>>(...), \c cast<list<auto>>(...), and their
    \c "or nothing" variants instead of raising a parse error.  These casts do not coerce values, so they
    were made parse-time errors in %Qore 3.0; this option restores the old accepting behavior for backward
    compatibility.  New code should use \c cast<hash>(...) / \c cast<list>(...) for hard container
    assertions instead.  Not included in @ref modern "%modern".

    @since %Qore 3.0

    <hr>
    @section broken-cast %broken-cast

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-cast</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-cast</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_CAST

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-0.9.4 broken @ref cast "cast<>" operator handling where @ref nothing would be accepted without an error

    @see @ref correct-cast "%correct-cast"

    @since %Qore 0.9.4

    <hr>
    @section broken-int-assignments %broken-int-assignments

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-int-assignments</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-int-assignments</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_INT_ASSIGNMENTS

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-0.8.12 broken @ref int_type "int" and @ref softint_type "softint" runtime handling where type errors were ignored at runtime.

    @see @ref correct-int-assignments "%correct-int-assignments"

    @since %Qore 0.8.12

    <hr>
    @section broken-list-parsing %broken-list-parsing

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-list-parsing</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-list-parsing</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_LIST_PARSING

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-0.8.12 broken list parsing where certain lists without parentheses would be rewritten to make top-level statements like <tt>list l = 1, 2, 3;</tt> valid.

    @see @ref correct-list-parsing "%correct-list-parsing"

    @since %Qore 0.8.12

    <hr>
    @section broken-list-range %broken-list-range

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-list-range</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-list-range</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_LIST_RANGE

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-2.0 broken list range operator where out-of-range values would result in returning @ref NOTHING
    values, breaking strongly-typed lists and contrary to the behavior of the same operator on a string

    @see @ref correct-list-range "%correct-list-range"

    @since %Qore 2.0

    <hr>
    @section broken-logic-precedence %broken-logic-precedence

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-logic-precedence</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-logic-precedence</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_LOGIC_PRECEDENCE

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-0.8.12 precedence where the \ref logical_and_operator "&&" had the same precedence as \ref logical_or_operator "||" and all three bitwise operators (
    \ref bitwise_and_operator "&", \ref bitwise_or_operator "|", \ref bitwise_xor_operator "^") shared the same precedence.

    An example of impact of @ref broken-logic-precedence "%broken-logic-precedence" directive:

    - with @ref broken-logic-precedence "%broken-logic-precedence":
    @code
    True \|\| False && False = False
    1 \| 2 ^ 3 & 5 = 0
    @endcode

    - without @ref broken-logic-precedence "%broken-logic-precedence" (or with @ref correct-logic-precedence "%correct-logic-precedence"):
    @code
    True \|\| False && False = True
    1 \| 2 ^ 3 & 5 = 3
    @endcode

    @see @ref correct-logic-precedence "%correct-logic-precedence"

    @since %Qore 0.8.12

    <hr>
    @section broken-namespace-resolution %broken-namespace-resolution

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-namespace-resolution</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-namespace-resolution</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_NAMESPACE_RESOLUTION

    @par Description:
    Enables legacy namespace resolution behavior where symbol resolution prefers the shortest path and can
    allow user-defined symbols to shadow symbols in the @ref Qore "Qore" namespace.

    @see @ref correct-namespace-resolution "%correct-namespace-resolution"

    @since %Qore 3.0

    <hr>
    @section broken-loop-statement %broken-loop-statement

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-loop-statement</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-broken-loop-statement</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_LOOP_STATEMENT

    @par Description:
    Use old pre-0.8.13 handling of break/continue: continue statements in a switch behave like a break statement,
    both @ref break "break" and @ref continue "continue" behave like @ref return "return" when used outside of a loop.

    @since %Qore 0.8.13

    <hr>
    @section broken-narrowed-types %broken-narrowed-types

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-narrowed-types</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-narrowed-types</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_NARROWED_TYPES

    @par Description:
    Disables parse-time type narrowing for variables declared with \c auto, \c hash<auto>, and \c list<auto>.
    When this option is set, the compiler will not narrow variable types based on assignments, and type
    checking will be deferred to runtime.

    This option is provided for backwards compatibility with code that relies on the old behavior where
    \c auto variables could be assigned values of different types without parse-time errors.

    @par Example:
    Without this option, the following code would fail at parse time:
    @code{.py}
auto val = 1;        # val is narrowed to 'int'
val = "string";      # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR without %broken-narrowed-types
    @endcode

    With \c %%broken-narrowed-types, the code parses successfully and type checking occurs at runtime.

    @see @ref correct-narrowed-types "%correct-narrowed-types"
    @see @ref auto_no_narrow "auto!" syntax for per-variable control

    @since %Qore 3.0

    <hr>
    @section broken-operators %broken-operators

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-operators</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-pbroken-operators</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_OPERATORS

    @par Description:
    Accept spaces in the following multi-character operators for backwards compatibility with %Qore before 0.8.12:
    - @ref logical_less_than_or_equals_operator "<="
    - @ref logical_greater_than_or_equals_operator ">="
    - @ref logical_comparison_operator "<=>"
    - @ref logical_absolute_equality_operator "==="
    - @ref logical_absolute_inequality_operator "!=="
    - @ref logical_and_operator "&&"
    - @ref logical_or_operator "||"
    - @ref plus_equals_operator "+="
    - @ref minus_equals_operator "-="
    - @ref and_equals_operator "&="
    - @ref or_equals_operator "|="
    - @ref modulo_equals_operator "%="
    - @ref multiply_equals_operator "*="
    - @ref divide_equals_operator "/="
    - @ref xor_equals_operator "^="
    - @ref shift_left_equals_operator "<<="
    - @ref shift_right_equals_operator ">>="
    - @ref logical_inequality_operator "!="
    - @ref shift_right_operator ">>"
    - @ref shift_left_operator "<<"
    .
    For example, when this parse directive is not set, the following is a bug:
    @code{.py}
int i = 1;
i+ =4;
    @endcode
    When this parse directive is set; the above code is accepted.

    @see @ref correct-operators "%correct-operators"

    @since %Qore 0.8.12

    <hr>
    @section broken-range %broken-range

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%broken-range</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-pbroken-range</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_BROKEN_RANGE

    @par Description:
    Include the upper limit in @ref Qore::range() "range()" and @ref Qore::xrange() "xrange()" as with %Qore before 0.9.5

    @see @ref correct-range "%correct-range"

    @since %Qore 0.9.5

