    @section modern %modern

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%modern</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-set-parse-option=modern, -M</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_MODERN

    @par Description:
    Sets @ref new-style "%new-style" (@ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs" and @ref assume-local "%assume-local"), @ref require-types "%require-types", @ref strict-args "%strict-args", @ref strong-encapsulation "%strong-encapsulation", @ref no-summarize "%no-summarize", @ref streaming-any "%streaming-any", @ref negative-offsets "%negative-offsets", and enables all non-strict warnings.

    This is the recommended parse directive for modern %Qore programming. It combines all the best practices into a single directive.

    Because @ref streaming-any "%streaming-any" is enabled, \c any is parsed as the @ref streaming_any "any streaming operator" in expression contexts while remaining available as the @ref any_type "any" type name in declarations for compatibility. In @ref modern "%modern" code, use @ref auto_type "auto" for unrestricted declarations unless complex runtime type stripping is specifically required.

    @par File Extension:
    Starting with %Qore 3.0, files with any extension except \c .q automatically enable \c %%modern mode without requiring any parse directive. This includes conventional extensions such as \c .qr, \c .qc, \c .qm, \c .qtest, \c .qsd, \c .qfd, \c .qrd, \c .qore, as well as files with no extension at all. Only files whose filename ends in exactly \c .q stay in legacy mode by default, preserving backward compatibility for existing scripts.

    @code{.py}
    # hello.qr - no parse directives needed!
    string sub greet(string name) {
        return "Hello, " + name + "!";
    }
    printf("%s\n", greet("World"));
    @endcode

    @par Environment Override:
    Setting the \c QORE_OLD_STYLE_DEFAULT environment variable to any non-empty value other than \c "0" disables the extension-based auto-enable globally; under this override, only the \c %%modern parse directive, the \c -M command-line flag, or an explicit @ref Qore::PO_MODERN programmatic option will enable modern mode. This escape hatch is intended for users rolling legacy code bases forward without renaming every source file to \c .q.

    @note
    - All examples in the %Qore documentation assume @ref modern "%modern" mode.
    - When using this option, the lack of \c "$" characters in variable names makes it necessary to declare local variables before using them.
    - All declarations require type information.
    - Use \c "any (expr)" for the streaming operator in syntactically ambiguous positions such as direct function-call arguments.

    @since %Qore 3.0

    <hr>
    @section new-style %new-style

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%new-style</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-new-style, -n</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::PO_NEW_STYLE

    @par Description:
    Sets both @ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs" and @ref assume-local "%assume-local". These two options together make programming in %Qore superficially more like programming in C++ or Java programs; use this if you dislike programming with the \c "$" sign, for example.

    @see @ref old-style "%old-style", @ref modern "%modern"

    @note
    - For new code, consider using @ref modern "%modern" instead, which includes @ref new-style "%new-style" plus additional recommended options.
    - when using this option (or @ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs) the lack of \c "$" characters in variable names makes it necessary to declare local variables before using them

    @since %Qore 0.8.1

