    @section no-any-operator %no-any-operator

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%no-any-operator</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-set-parse-option=no-any-operator</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::QoreParseOptions::NO_ANY_OPERATOR

    @par Description:
    Disables the @ref streaming_any "any" streaming operator keyword. The @ref any_type "any" type name remains available in all type declaration positions. This directive overrides @ref streaming-any "%streaming-any" and the @ref modern "%modern" implication for \c any.

    Use this option only for source compatibility with code where unparenthesized \c any expressions must never be interpreted as streaming operators.

    @since %Qore 3.0

    <hr>
    @section streaming-any %streaming-any

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%streaming-any</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-set-parse-option=streaming-any</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::QoreParseOptions::STREAMING_ANY

    @par Description:
    Treats ambiguous \c any expressions as the @ref streaming_any "any" streaming operator in expression contexts. This means \c "any values" is parsed as "does \c values yield at least one element" in expression-only positions such as after \c return or an assignment operator. The @ref any_type "any" type name remains available in declaration contexts such as variable declarations, parameters, return types, and member declarations for compatibility. New modern declarations should use @ref auto_type "auto" instead.

    In syntactic positions that can also be declaration or parameter lists, such as direct function-call arguments, write the source expression explicitly as \c "any (values)". Predicate forms such as \c "any $1 > 0, values" and \c "any isValid($1), values" are parsed as the streaming operator when it is enabled.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%streaming-any

bool sub hasValues(list<int> values) {
    auto saved = values;     # auto is preferred for unrestricted modern declarations
    return any values;       # any is the streaming operator in expression context
}

bool sub accept(bool value) {
    return value;
}

bool sub hasValuesArg(list<int> values) {
    return accept(any (values)); # explicit source expression in a call argument
}
    @endcode

    @note
    - @ref modern "%modern" implies this option.
    - Use @ref no-any-operator "%no-any-operator" to disable the \c any streaming operator entirely.
    - The \c any type is retained for backward compatibility but is not recommended for new modern declarations; use @ref auto_type "auto" unless complex type stripping is required.

    @since %Qore 3.0

