    @section negative-offsets %negative-offsets

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%negative-offsets</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    <tt>-</tt><tt>-set-parse-option=negative-offsets</tt>

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    @ref Qore::QoreParseOptions::NEGATIVE_OFFSETS

    @par Description:
    Interprets negative indexes and slice endpoints for lists, strings, binary values, and buffers as offsets from the end of the container.  For example, \c value[-1] addresses the last element, and \c value[-2..] addresses the last two elements.  This option affects single indexes, range endpoints, list/string/binary selector lists, and lvalue assignment or removal where a single valid negative sequence offset is used.

    Invalid negative indexes remain invalid after normalization.  Reads return @ref nothing for out-of-range single indexes; lvalue assignment and single-index removal raise the same index exceptions as other invalid negative lvalue indexes.

    This option is implied by @ref modern "%modern". Use @ref no-negative-offsets "%no-negative-offsets" after @ref modern "%modern" to preserve legacy negative-index behavior in a compatibility file.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%negative-offsets

list<int> values = (10, 20, 30, 40);
int last = values[-1];              # 40
list<int> tail = values[-2..];      # (30, 40)
list<int> middle = values[1..-2];   # (20, 30)

string suffix = "qore"[-2..];       # "re"
binary b = <102030>[-2..-1];        # <2030>
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @see @ref list_element_operator "[] operator", @ref no-negative-offsets "%no-negative-offsets"

    <hr>
    @section no-negative-offsets %no-negative-offsets

    @par Parse Directive:
    <tt>%%no-negative-offsets</tt>

    @par Command Line:
    n/a

    @par Parse Option Constant:
    n/a (clears @ref Qore::QoreParseOptions::NEGATIVE_OFFSETS)

    @par Description:
    Clears @ref negative-offsets "%negative-offsets" in the current parse context. Use this directive after @ref modern "%modern" when a source file depends on legacy negative-index behavior, for example expecting list and binary single indexes below zero to return @ref nothing instead of addressing from the end.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%no-negative-offsets

assertEq(NOTHING, (10, 20, 30)[-1]);
assertEq((), (10, 20, 30)[..-1]);
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @see @ref negative-offsets "%negative-offsets"

